## Key Findings

- Senior citizens have a higher churn rate than non-senior citizens.
- Month-to-month customers are more likely to churn than customers with one-year or two-year contracts.
- Customers with longer tenure generally show lower churn.
- Customers without additional services such as online security, online backup, device protection, and tech support show higher churn in the groups analyzed.
- Fiber optic customers contribute significant revenue but also show a higher churn pattern.
- Electronic check customers show a higher churn pattern compared with some other payment methods.
- Two-year contract customers contribute the highest revenue among retained customers.
- High Value and Medium Value customers represent most of the retained customer base.
- The High Value–High Risk group contains 1,083 customers and contributes approximately $2.81M in revenue.
- Many customers on month-to-month contracts do not have additional services such as online security, device protection, and tech support.

## Recommendations

- Focus on retaining High Value–High Risk customers first.
- Encourage month-to-month customers to move to longer-term contracts where appropriate.
- Consider suitable packages that include online security, online backup, device protection, and tech support.
- Give additional attention to senior citizen customers who show high churn risk.
- Monitor fiber optic customers because they contribute significant revenue but also show higher churn.
- Pay attention to customers using electronic checks because of their higher churn pattern.
- Keep long-term customers satisfied because they provide more stable revenue.

